Q: How can I use Material UI 7 with Tailwind CSS 4? A: <p><strong>TL;DR</strong>: Omit <code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code>. Instead, import Tailwind CSS manually with the correct layer order and include the <code>mui</code> layer. See the end of the answer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But the button on page.tsx has rounded corners. Why?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79996658/15167500">Rico</a> mentioned, MUI and Tailwind CSS conflict in several places and can override each other's styles.</p>
<p>As with many other framework integrations, you need to ensure:</p>
<ul>
<li>correct <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Styling_basics/Cascade_layers#review_of_the_cascade_concept" rel="nofollow noreferrer">layer order</a> (<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@layer" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>@layer</code></a>)</li>
<li>correct class names, using prefixes where necessary; for example, <a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/upgrade-guide#using-a-prefix" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Tailwind CSS supports prefixing</a> its utilities</li>
<li>correct handling of base styles, either enabling or disabling them as appropriate; for example, Tailwind CSS injects its <a href="https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/blob/v4.3.3/packages/tailwindcss/preflight.css" rel="nofollow noreferrer">own base styles</a> by default through <a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/preflight" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Preflight</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Here are some previous integration examples that cover these concepts and may help you build this setup yourself:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79561845/15167500">Can use both Tailwind CSS and Bootstrap at the same time?</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79758663/15167500">TailwindCSS v4 utilities are too weak to override the styling of Ant Design v5 components</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79674300/15167500">chakra-ui overwrites TailwindCSS utilities/classes</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79836789/15167500">How to prevent TailwindCSS plugins from overriding third-party component styles</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79807110/15167500">Radix UI's global CSS cannot be overridden with TailwindCSS utilities - only with !important</a></li>
</ul>
<p>The following inline example shows the border radius you mentioned:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="true" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@tailwindcss/browser@4"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script type="module"&gt;
import React from "https://esm.sh/react@19";
import { createRoot } from "https://esm.sh/react-dom@19/client";
import Button from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/Button";
import GlobalStyles from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/GlobalStyles";
import { StyledEngineProvider } from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/styles";

function App() {
  return React.createElement(
    StyledEngineProvider,
    { enableCssLayer: true },

    React.createElement(
      "div",
      { className: "p-8 flex flex-col gap-4" },

      React.createElement(
        Button,
        {
          variant: "contained",
          className:
            "rounded-none bg-sky-500 px-8 py-4 text-white hover:bg-sky-700"
        },
        "MUI + Tailwind CSS"
      ),

      React.createElement(
        Button,
        {
          variant: "contained",
        },
        "Only MUI"
      ),

      React.createElement(
        "div",
        {
          variant: "contained",
          className:
            "rounded-none bg-sky-500 px-8 py-4 text-white hover:bg-sky-700"
        },
        "Only Tailwind CSS"
      ),
    )
  );
}

createRoot(document.getElementById("root")).render(
  React.createElement(App)
);
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Looking at the generated CSS, you can also see the <code>mui</code> layer appearing, as you described. Tailwind CSS mainly provides utility class styles, while the <code>mui</code> layer contains complete component styling with many rules. Because of that, we need to make the complete MUI component rules stronger than Tailwind CSS utilities. Let's see:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="true" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* Added correctly layer ordering */
@layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities;</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@tailwindcss/browser@4"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script type="module"&gt;
import React from "https://esm.sh/react@19";
import { createRoot } from "https://esm.sh/react-dom@19/client";
import Button from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/Button";
import GlobalStyles from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/GlobalStyles";
import { StyledEngineProvider } from "https://esm.sh/@mui/material@7.3.9/styles";

function App() {
  return React.createElement(
    StyledEngineProvider,
    { enableCssLayer: true },

    React.createElement(
      "div",
      { className: "p-8 flex flex-col gap-4" },

      React.createElement(
        Button,
        {
          variant: "contained",
          className:
            "rounded-none bg-sky-500 px-8 py-4 text-white hover:bg-sky-700"
        },
        "MUI + Tailwind CSS"
      ),

      React.createElement(
        Button,
        {
          variant: "contained",
        },
        "Only MUI"
      ),

      React.createElement(
        "div",
        {
          variant: "contained",
          className:
            "rounded-none bg-sky-500 px-8 py-4 text-white hover:bg-sky-700"
        },
        "Only Tailwind CSS"
      ),
    )
  );
}

createRoot(document.getElementById("root")).render(
  React.createElement(App)
);
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Why did it work without issues? In Tailwind Play, default <code>@layer</code> order is overridden automatically, but that is not necessarily the case in a regular project.</p>
<p><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code> includes Tailwind CSS's default <code>@layer</code> order itself. If you need a different order, best approach is to avoid <code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code> entirely and import Tailwind's parts manually.</p>
<p>So in <code>globals.css</code>, use this instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* Omit this */
/* @import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;; */

/* Use this instead, and make sure the layer order is correct and that the &quot;mui&quot; layer is included */
@layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities;

@import &quot;tailwindcss/theme.css&quot; layer(theme);
@import &quot;tailwindcss/preflight.css&quot; layer(base);
@import &quot;tailwindcss/utilities.css&quot; layer(utilities);
</code></pre>
<p>Note: I added <code>enableCssLayer</code> by default since it was mentioned in the question.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { StyledEngineProvider } from &quot;@mui/material/styles&quot;;

&lt;StyledEngineProvider enableCssLayer&gt;
  {children}
&lt;/StyledEngineProvider&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The standalone example above uses <code>StyledEngineProvider</code> because it runs as a plain client-side React application, not inside Next.js App Router.</p>
<p>In a real Next.js 16 App Router project, use <code>AppRouterCacheProvider</code> instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-tsx prettyprint-override"><code>import { AppRouterCacheProvider } from &quot;@mui/material-nextjs/v16-appRouter&quot;;

&lt;AppRouterCacheProvider options={{ enableCssLayer: true }}&gt;
  {children}
&lt;/AppRouterCacheProvider&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Both approaches serve the same purpose here: they place MUI-generated styles into the <code>mui</code> cascade layer. Difference is integration environment: <code>StyledEngineProvider</code> for standalone React/SPAs, <code>AppRouterCacheProvider</code> for Next.js App Router.</p>

Q: How to read a specific text file format in C A: <p>Regarding: <em>&quot;Oh no as in how can I read a file but like any file that has a file format that is ending in .uc or something...&quot;</em></p>
<p>Start easy: Require the user of your program to supply a list of the files that the program should read. Your <code>main</code> could look something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc &lt; 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, &quot;USAGE: %s uc-files...\n&quot;, argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    for (int idx = 1; idx &lt; argc; ++idx) {
        FILE* fp = fopen(argv[idx], &quot;r&quot;); // or &quot;rb&quot; if .uc is a binary file format
        if (fp) {
            // process the file here
            fclose(fp);
        } else {
            // failed to open the file so print an error:
            fprintf(stderr, &quot;ERROR: Failed opening \&quot;%s\&quot;\n&quot;, argv[idx]);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I check whether a string contains only digits in Go? A: <p>Two king of solution have been proposed above:
the first considers the string as a whole, the second checks rune by rune whether is an unicode digit or not. Some solution lack the ability to intercept the + and - sign as a non gidit value.</p>
<p>This is a complete example of a 'check rune by rune' solution, inspired by a previous one and refined.</p>
<pre><code>package main

import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;unicode&quot;
)


func main() {


    OnlyDigits := func(str string) bool {
        if len(str) == 0 {
            return false
        }
        for _, r := range str {
            if !unicode.IsDigit(r) {
                return false // not an unicode digit -&gt; false
            }
        }
        return true
    }

    // usage:
    str1 := &quot;-123&quot;

    bool1 := OnlyDigits(str1)
    if !bool1 {
        fmt.Println(&quot;error, not only digits in&quot;, str1)
    } else {
        fmt.Println(&quot;ok, only digits in&quot;, str1)
    }
    // error, not only digits in -123

}
</code></pre>

